In Kubernetes, imagePullPolicy tells Kubernetes when it should pull the container image from the container registry.

Example
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: myapp
spec:
  containers:
    - name: app
      image: nginx:1.25
      imagePullPolicy: IfNotPresent

There are 3 values:

imagePullPolicy	     What Kubernetes does
Always            	Pull/check the image from registry every time the container starts
IfNotPresent      	Pull only if the image is not already on the node
Never	               Never pull; image must already exist on the node

1. IfNotPresent
Suppose Node 1 already has:  nginx:1.25

Pod starts:
Pod
 ↓
Node 1
 ↓
nginx:1.25 already exists
 ↓
Use existing image

No download is needed.

If the image doesn't exist:
Pod
 ↓
Node 1
 ↓
nginx:1.25 NOT present
 ↓
Pull from Docker Hub/registry
 ↓
Start container



2. Always
image: nginx:latest
imagePullPolicy: Always

When the container starts, Kubernetes checks the registry for the image.

Pod starts
   ↓
Check registry
   ↓
Pull/check image
   ↓
Start container

This is commonly useful with latest when you want Kubernetes to check for a newer image.



3. Never
image: nginx:1.25
imagePullPolicy: Never

Kubernetes says:  "Don't download anything. If the image isn't already on this node, the container cannot start."
So if the image is missing, you can get an image-related failure such as:

ErrImageNeverPull
Important interview point
imagePullPolicy applies to the node where the Pod is running.

For example:
Node 1
 └── nginx:1.25 exists

Node 2
 └── nginx:1.25 does NOT exist

If your Pod gets scheduled to Node 2 and policy is IfNotPresent, Kubernetes pulls the image on Node 2.

Simple meaning:imagePullPolicy = "Should Kubernetes download the container image, or use the image already available on the node?"



If you change the image tag, for example from 1 to 2, Kubernetes treats them as different images.

Example:

image: nginx:1
imagePullPolicy: IfNotPresent

Suppose Node already has:

nginx:1

Kubernetes uses it.

Now you change to:

image: nginx:2

The node has:

nginx:1   ✅
nginx:2   ❌

Because nginx:2 is not present, Kubernetes pulls nginx:2 from the registry.

Pod
 ↓
image: nginx:2
 ↓
Does Node have nginx:2?
 ↓
NO
 ↓
Pull nginx:2 from registry
 ↓
Start container

So:

nginx:1 → nginx:2 = different image tag → Kubernetes needs nginx:2 available on that node.

If you use IfNotPresent, it will pull 2 only if 2 is not already on the node.

Same tag, but the code inside the image is different can happen.

For example, you build your application:

First build
myapp:v1
    ↓
Code: "Hello World"

Push it to the registry.

Later you change the code:

myapp:v1
    ↓
Code: "Hello Kubernetes"

and push again using the same tag v1.

Now the tag is still:

myapp:v1

but the actual image content is different.

What happens depends on imagePullPolicy

IfNotPresent:

Node already has myapp:v1
        ↓
Use existing myapp:v1
        ↓
❌ New code may NOT be downloaded

Always:

Pod starts
   ↓
Check registry for myapp:v1
   ↓
Find current image
   ↓
Pull/use the current image
   ↓
✅ New code

That's why using mutable tags such as latest can be confusing.

Better practice

Instead of:

myapp:v1

and replacing its code, use a new tag for every build:

myapp:v1
myapp:v2
myapp:v3

Then Kubernetes clearly knows:

v1 = old code
v2 = new code
v3 = newer code

Key point: A tag like v1 is just a name. The actual image content can change if you push a new image with the same tag.




imagePullPolicy = "Should Kubernetes download the container image, or use the image already available on the node?"
